return A.c(A.nQ(m,"how_to_play",'<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang="en">\n<head>\n  <meta charset="UTF-8">\n  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">\n  <title>T-G-Apps Cribbage How to Play</title>\n  <style>\n    body {\n      margin: 0;\n      padding: 16px;\n      background: #f6f1e4;\n      color: #111827;\n      font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n      font-size: 16px;\n      line-height: 1.45;\n    }\n\n    .tgapps-policy-page {\n      width: 100%;\n      max-width: 900px;\n      margin: 0 auto;\n    }\n\n    .hero,\n    .card {\n      display: block;\n      width: auto;\n      margin: 0 0 14px 0;\n      padding: 14px;\n      border-radius: 12px;\n      background: #ffffff;\n      border: 1px solid #D4AF37;\n    }\n\n    .hero {\n      background: #063a25;\n      color: #ffffff;\n    }\n\n    .badge {\n      display: none;\n    }\n\n    h1, h2, h3 {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 0;\n      color: #063a25;\n    }\n\n    .hero h1,\n    .hero h2 {\n      color: #ffd966;\n    }\n\n    p {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 0;\n    }\n\n    ul {\n      margin: 0 0 10px 22px;\n      padding: 0;\n    }\n\n    li {\n      margin-bottom: 5px;\n    }\n\n    a {\n      color: #0b63ce;\n      word-break: break-word;\n    }\n\n    .footer {\n      text-align: center;\n      color: #ffffff;\n      background: #063a25;\n      padding: 12px;\n      border-radius: 12px;\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <div class="tgapps-policy-page">\n    <section class="hero">\n      <h1>T-G-Apps LLC</h1>\n      <h2>T-G-Apps Cribbage How to Play</h2>\n      <p class="subtitle">Peg your way around the board: score in the play and in the show, and be the first to reach the end.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>1. Goal</h3>\n      <p>Be the first player to reach 121 points (or 61 in a short game). Points come from playing cards in turn (pegging) and from counting your hand after the play (the show). The game ends the moment someone reaches the target, even in the middle of a hand.</p>\n      <p>With four players, partners share one score and win together.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>2. Setup Options</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Number of players: </strong>2, 3 or 4. With 4 players, players 1 and 3 are partners against players 2 and 4.</li>\n        <li><strong>Players: </strong>each player has a name and a type. Player 1 is always a person; tap the Type button to switch any other player between Human and Robot. If more than one person plays, pass the phone when the game asks you to.</li>\n        <li><strong>Points to win: </strong>121 for a full game, or 61 for a short game.</li>\n        <li><strong>Five-card cribbage: </strong>the original game, for 2 players (see section 7).</li>\n        <li><strong>Skunks: </strong>a big win counts as 2 or 3 games (see section 7).</li>\n        <li><strong>Muggins: </strong>you count your own hand and crib in the show (see section 5).</li>\n        <li><strong>Music: </strong>Background music on or off, and its volume.</li>\n        <li><strong>Robot skill: </strong>Beginner, Intermediate or Expert. All robots play at the same level.</li>\n        <li><strong>Show hints: </strong>On or off. When it is off, there is no Hint button and no outlined cards.</li>\n      </ul>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>3. The Deal and the Crib</h3>\n      <p>With two players each gets 6 cards and puts 2 in the crib. With three players each gets 5 cards and puts 1 in the crib, and one card from the deck goes in too. With four players each gets 5 cards and puts 1 in the crib. The crib always has 4 cards and belongs to the dealer. The deal passes to the left each hand.</p>\n      <p>Tap cards to pick them, then press Put the Cards in the Crib. Give your own crib good cards; keep good cards away from another player\'s crib.</p>\n      <p>Then a starter card is cut and turned face up. If it is a jack, the dealer pegs 2 for his heels.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>4. The Play (Pegging)</h3>\n      <p>Starting left of the dealer, players take turns playing one card face up, adding its value to the count. Face cards count 10, aces 1, other cards their number. The count may not go over 31.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Fifteen: </strong>make the count 15 for 2.</li>\n        <li><strong>Thirty-one: </strong>make the count exactly 31 for 2.</li>\n        <li><strong>Pairs: </strong>play the same rank as the card before for 2; three in a row for 6; four for 12.</li>\n        <li><strong>Runs: </strong>when the last 3 or more cards make a run in any order (such as 5, 3, 4), score 1 for each card.</li>\n        <li><strong>Go: </strong>if you cannot play without going over 31, you say go and the others keep playing. When nobody can play, the last player to play pegs 1 and the count starts again at 0.</li>\n        <li><strong>Last card: </strong>the player who plays the last card of the hand pegs 1 (unless it made 31).</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The game says go for you when you cannot play. Tap a card, then press Play. Cards you cannot play are dimmed.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>5. The Show</h3>\n      <p>After the play, each hand is counted with the starter, starting left of the dealer. The dealer counts last, then counts the crib.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Fifteens: </strong>2 for each different set of cards that adds to 15.</li>\n        <li><strong>Pairs: </strong>2 for each pair (three of a kind is 3 pairs, 6 points; four of a kind is 12).</li>\n        <li><strong>Runs: </strong>1 for each card in a run of 3 or more. Aces are low: A-2-3 is a run, Q-K-A is not. Duplicate cards make more runs: 3-4-4-5 is two runs of 3.</li>\n        <li><strong>Flush: </strong>4 cards of one suit in the hand for 4, or 5 with the starter. The crib only scores a flush of all 5.</li>\n        <li><strong>His nobs: </strong>the jack of the same suit as the starter, in the hand or crib, for 1.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>The best possible hand is 29: three 5s and the jack of the starter suit, with the fourth 5 as the starter.</p>\n      <p><strong>Muggins: </strong>when this option is on, you count your own hand and crib. Set your count with \u2212 and +, then press Claim. If you claim too few points, the next opponent calls \u201cMuggins!\u201d and takes the points you missed. If you claim too many, you score only the real count. Robots always count right.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>6. The Board, Hints and Robots</h3>\n      <p>The board at the top shows each player\'s pegs: the front peg at the score and the back peg at the score before the last points.</p>\n      <p>Press Hint for advice on which cards to put in the crib or which card to play. The recommended cards are outlined in white. Hints always give the Expert robot\'s advice, with its reasons and, when they help, its numbers: what the kept cards are worth with the starter, what the crib gains or gives away and, near the end of the game, the chance to win. Beginner robots keep the cards worth the most with any starter, take points in the play and avoid leaving an easy 15 or 31. Intermediate and Expert robots work out many ways the hidden cards could lie, think ahead in the play, and play to win the game, not only the hand.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>7. Partners, Five-Card Cribbage and Skunks</h3>\n      <p><strong>Partners: </strong>with four players, partners sit across from each other: players 1 and 3 against players 2 and 4. Partners share one score and one track on the board, and both partners\' hands count for the team. The crib belongs to the dealer\'s team.</p>\n      <p><strong>Five-card cribbage: </strong>each player gets 5 cards and puts 2 in the crib, keeping 3. At the start of the game the first non-dealer pegs 3, to make up for the dealer\'s first crib. The play has only one count: when the count reaches 31 or nobody can play, the play ends, and any cards left stay in the hands. A hand of 3 cards scores a flush of 3, or 4 with the starter. The game is to 61.</p>\n      <p><strong>Skunks: </strong>if the loser has not passed the skunk line (91 in a game to 121, 31 in a game to 61), the win counts as 2 games. In a game to 121, a loser with 60 points or less is double skunked: the win counts as 3 games. On the board for a game to 121, the red line is the skunk line. Skunks are not used with 3 players.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>8. Games History</h3>\n      <p>Every finished game is saved with each player\'s final score. Games History lists the newest games first.</p>\n      <p>With skunks on, a skunk or double skunk is noted under the winner.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <!-- tgapps-room-how-to-play 9 -->\n\n    <section class="card">\n      <h3>Responsible Play</h3>\n      <p>T-G-Apps Cribbage is an entertainment game. It does not offer real-money gambling, prizes, wagers or cash-out functionality.</p>\n    </section>\n\n    <p class="footer">\xa9 2026 T-G-Apps LLC. All rights reserved.</p>\n  </div>\n</body>\n</html>\n',"tgapps_cribbage_engine"),$async$LF)
